# Rank 1540 / CVE-2024-55470: Oqtane's settings controller defaulted
# its authorization flag to true and only computed a real decision for
# one permission name, so every other permission (View among them)
# passed open for any authenticated user (broken access control,
# CWE-862; PR #4878 "User Settings should only be accessible to
# individual users or administrators"; the task list's nocommit flag
# was advisory-side absence). The User-entity branch of IsAuthorized
# in Oqtane.Server/Controllers/SettingController.cs did `authorized =
# true; if (permissionName == PermissionNames.Edit) { authorized =
# _userPermissions.IsAuthorized(User, _alias.SiteId, entityName, -1,
# PermissionNames.Write, RoleNames.Admin) ||
# (_userPermissions.GetUser(User).UserId == entityId); }`, and the
# listing filter checked only the Admin role and the caller's user id,
# missing the permission path entirely. Pool commit cdd03bf3d4 (the
# merge of PR #4878; parent e83399a is the vulnerable tree, and the
# merge's combined diff is empty, so the fix reads via `git diff
# e83399a cdd03bf3d4`) carries the controller change: the User-entity
# decision applies to every permission name unconditionally and the
# filter uses the same permission decision.
#
# ATTENTION, default-open authorization branches. The defect is an
# authorization variable that starts true and is narrowed in only one
# branch of a permission conditional -- decision-completeness over
# the permission vocabulary, the same family as ranks 1519
# (capability gates), 1535 (startup guards) and 1556 (sibling route
# groups): no rule can ask "does every permission name receive an
# authorization decision", and the default-true initialization is not
# an observable guard shape. A binding on Oqtane's own
# _userPermissions would detect this application rather than the
# weakness.
#
# Re-probed at the recheck span tip, which carries every definition
# and engine change shipped since the first pass (loaded:
# concepts=1007 rules=879 bindings=32983 reviews=678; binary built in
# the worktree, data directory resolving to the worktree's own
# vyql/). Measured 3x --cache off / timeout 180 / --max-ram 6GB per
# revision on the real checkout, vulnerable parent e83399a against
# fix cdd03bf3d4:
#
#   whole repository (javascript:14 csharp:554 bash:4 powershell:2
#   config:15 textpattern:579 files analysed) -- 309/309/309 findings
#   at the vulnerable revision, 309/309/309 at the fix, 154 at or
#   above high at each, deterministic. The finding set is identical
#   across the pair: the same 22 rule ids with the same counts (101
#   VYQL-CFG-014, 69 VYQL-PATH-001, 60 VYQL-AUTH-002, ...) and the
#   same 270 rule+source-location pairs. No finding names
#   SettingController.cs at either revision in the full findings view
#   (3x each). The flags view carries two
#   code.ObjectLookupByUserInput flags on the controller's
#   `GetUser(User).UserId` comparisons -- :275/:322 at the vulnerable
#   revision, :272/:318 at the fix, the same expressions on both
#   sides -- consumed by VYQL-AUTH-002, which reports nothing there
#   at either revision. vyql match on the controller: sources
#   Param/ExternalEntryInput and HttpInput on the action parameters,
#   sinks exactly those two ObjectLookupByUserInput labels, no
#   control labels at all.
#
# Path 1 does not convert: the AUTH additions since the first pass
# (VYQL-AUTH-160 through VYQL-AUTH-179) are tool-schema security
# overrides, result staging, admission and request-scope policy, host
# validation and ignored-authorization-result -- and every emitter of
# code.IgnoredAuthorizationResult keys on another application's own
# call names (canFlush, canPerformSystemActions, InternalClient), so
# none fires here; the one csharp emitter of the pinned
# code.SensitiveOperation keys on an unrelated MigrateEmail function.
# Path 2 was a hard zero in the full findings view, not a misread
# one, and the flags that do exist label both revisions identically.
# Path 3 is not shippable today, and the blocker is measured rather
# than supposed: the default-open construct (`authorized = true; if
# (permissionName == PermissionNames.Edit) {...}`) SURVIVES the fix
# in the same switch's `default:` custom-entity case, so a rule on
# the construct fires on the fixed revision of the fix's own file and
# no 1 -> 0 exists on the real checkout. Separating the User-entity
# case from the surviving default case requires keying on
# EntityNames.User or on the guarded branch's own argument layout
# (the -1 id, the GetUser disjunct) -- application vocabulary and the
# fixing commit's layout. The missing piece is a
# decision-completeness vocabulary, which is not a taint, resolution
# or pattern construct the engine mishandles, so no ENGINE-GAPS.md
# entry. No definition change shipped. Spec cve_rank1540 records the
# default-open User-entity branch, the surviving default-open
# custom-entity case, the unconditional fixed form and both listing
# filters, all asserting reject VYQL-AUTH-079: when
# decision-completeness vocabulary lands, the default-open blocks
# must flip to expect and the unconditional forms must stay reject.

test "rank1540: the User entity branch decides only for Edit"
  lang csharp
  file Oqtane.Server/Controllers/SettingController.cs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
  using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
  using Oqtane.Models;
  using Oqtane.Shared;
  using Oqtane.Security;

  namespace Oqtane.Controllers
  {
      [Route(ControllerRoutes.ApiRoute)]
      public class SettingController : Controller
      {
          private readonly IUserPermissions _userPermissions;
          private readonly Alias _alias;

          public SettingController(IUserPermissions userPermissions, IAliasAccessor aliasAccessor)
          {
              _userPermissions = userPermissions;
              _alias = aliasAccessor.Alias;
          }

          private bool IsAuthorized(string entityName, int entityId, string permissionName)
          {
              bool authorized = false;
              switch (entityName)
              {
                  case EntityNames.User:
                      authorized = true;
                      if (permissionName == PermissionNames.Edit)
                      {
                          authorized = _userPermissions.IsAuthorized(User, _alias.SiteId, entityName, -1, PermissionNames.Write, RoleNames.Admin)
                              || (_userPermissions.GetUser(User).UserId == entityId);
                      }
                      break;
                  default:
                      authorized = false;
                      break;
              }
              return authorized;
          }
      }
  }
  ```

